From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 21:51:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09282/11362] Honest reason label for a vacuous recall gate,
 and who actually wrote 71014c8af5

THE LABEL FIX. `decide_resample` returned the literal reason
`catastrophic_recall_tool_loop_repair` on every fire. That is true at the
shipped 0.6 threshold. It becomes false if the threshold is ever raised above
1.0 -- the recipe that turns the recall test vacuous so the effective condition
is trust-reject plus evidence floor plus budgets. In that configuration a repair
firing on a recall-1.0 / accuracy-0.0 engine would write "catastrophic recall"
into the artifact, which is the opposite of that cell's actual state, in the
field whose only job is to say why the repair ran. Now returns
`trust_rejected_tool_loop_repair` / `trust_rejected_resample` when the threshold
is vacuous, and keeps `catastrophic_recall_*` for a real one. Default behaviour
is unchanged and its tests are untouched.

ATTRIBUTION CORRECTION FOR 71014c8af5, recorded rather than amended.

That commit's message describes refinement rounds, the CEGIS loop and the
cross-level carry. It also contains, unmentioned, another agent's in-flight
work: 45 lines in arc_induction_tool_loop.py including the tool-loop NUDGE FIX
and the new `CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TOOL_STALL_TURNS` lever, plus 83 lines of tests
in test_arc_induction_tool_loop.py and 35 in test_arc_tool_loop_repair.py.

The nudge fix is not incidental, which is why this correction is worth a commit
of its own. It is the difference between a repair arm that fixed 0 of 3 cells
and one that fixed 5 of 7 to zero visible mismatches. An earlier conclusion --
that seeded repair gains nothing in the 0.6-0.95 marginal band, which had been
called independently confirmed three times -- was measured on the broken arm and
is withdrawn. Attributing that fix to a commit about refinement rounds would
send anyone bisecting the reversal to the wrong change.

HOW IT HAPPENED, since the mechanism is more useful than the apology. Staging
was done with explicit paths rather than `git add -A`, specifically to avoid
sweeping up unrelated work. Two files that looked unfamiliar were diffed and
attributed before staging. The tool-loop files were not, because they already
held this session's own nudge edit and were assumed to be wholly ours. Checking
the unfamiliar and trusting the familiar is the hole: `git add -A` was avoided
and the same outcome arrived anyway. The rule that would have caught it is to
diff every staged path, not every surprising one.

Nothing is lost -- the swept-up changes are correct, tested, and green at 55/55
across the four affected suites. Only the record was wrong, and this corrects it
forward rather than by rewriting history.

